n64recomp: count the instructions a basic block stands for

A game that paces itself against how long its own work took runs faster here
than it ever did on a console, because recompiled code is not the R4300. Banjo-
Tooie asks the video interface for a frame every two retraces, misses that
target about a third of the time on a console because a frame of its own code
does not fit in two, and never missed it here: a flat thirty frames a second
against a console's twenty-six, an attract mode that finished thirteen seconds
early, and none of its three loading pauses.

Giving those back means knowing how much processor went past, so the recompiler
now says. RECOMP_CYCLES(n) is emitted at the head of every basic block with that
block's instruction count -- a block being exactly the unit where counting once
is right, since every instruction in one runs or none of them does. A block
starts at the function's first instruction, at any address something branches
to, and after the delay slot of any branch or jump; that last one because the
instruction after a delay slot is reached whether the branch was taken or not,
which makes it the head of a line of its own.

The runtime decides what to do with the number, and by default nothing: the
budget it is compared against starts at the largest number there is, so the cost
is a comparison against a value that never changes and a branch never taken.

--- a/include/recomp.h
+++ b/include/recomp.h
@@ -461,6 +461,32 @@
 extern volatile unsigned recomp_preempt_wanted;
 void recomp_preempt(uint8_t *rdram, unsigned status_reg);
 #define YIELD_POINT() if (recomp_preempt_wanted) { recomp_preempt(rdram, ctx->status_reg); }
+
+// How much of the console's processor this code has used up.
+//
+// A game paces itself against the video interface: it does a frame's work and
+// then waits for however many retraces it asked for. Whether it gets them is a
+// question about how long the work took, and on the console the work takes
+// long enough that a game often misses the retrace it was aiming at and shows
+// the same picture for one more sixtieth of a second. Recompiled, the same
+// work takes almost none, so the game never misses -- and a game that never
+// misses runs its own clock faster than the console ever ran it, which is a
+// demo that plays out too quickly and a loading pause that is not there.
+//
+// So the runtime keeps a count of the instructions this code stands for, and
+// can hold it to the rate a 93.75 MHz R4300 would have managed. The count is
+// added once per basic block rather than once per instruction, because a block
+// is a straight line: whoever enters it runs all of it.
+//
+// `recomp_cycle_budget` is the count at which the runtime wants to be called.
+// It is the largest number there is when nothing is holding the game back, so
+// the test costs a comparison against a register and a branch that is never
+// taken.
+extern uint64_t recomp_cycles;
+extern uint64_t recomp_cycle_budget;
+void recomp_cycle_spend(void);
+#define RECOMP_CYCLES(n) \
+    do { recomp_cycles += (n); if (recomp_cycles >= recomp_cycle_budget) { recomp_cycle_spend(); } } while (0)
 
 // The function signature for all recompiler output functions.
 typedef void (recomp_func_t)(uint8_t* rdram, recomp_context* ctx);
